          "court": "Illinois Supreme Court",
          "year": 2004,
          "date_decided": "2004-10-28",
          "holding": "The court held that at an adjudicatory hearing the circuit court must focus solely on whether the child was neglected rather than apportion blame among parents, and, finding the State had failed to prove neglect as to Arthur Jr., reversed the circuit court and dismissed the amended wardship petition.",
          "relevance": "Sets the burden and the sole question the circuit court may decide when the agency files a wardship petition in Illinois.",
          "quote": "It is the burden of the State to prove allegations of neglect by a preponderance of the evidence.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1988960/people-v-arthur-h/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1988960
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re A.W., Jr.",
          "citation": "231 Ill. 2d 241, 897 N.E.2d 733 (2008)",
          "court": "Illinois Supreme Court",
          "year": 2008,
          "date_decided": "2008-10-17",
          "holding": "The court reversed the appellate court and reinstated the neglect adjudication, holding that the circuit court properly considered all eight incidents of the father's violent and erratic conduct, not only those occurring in the children's presence.",
          "relevance": "Establishes the evidentiary frame for an injurious-environment petition built on a course of parental conduct.",
          "quote": "An adjudication of neglect is to be reviewed based on the totality of the evidence.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/3135466/people-v-pw/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 3135466
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Stanley v. Illinois",
          "citation": "405 U.S. 645 (1972)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1972,
          "date_decided": "1972-04-03",
          "holding": "The Court held that Illinois violated due process and equal protection by declaring the children of an unwed father dependent without any hearing on his fitness, and reversed the Illinois Supreme Court.",
          "relevance": "An Illinois case: the State cannot obtain wardship of a child without giving each parent a fitness hearing.",
          "quote": "We have concluded that all Illinois parents are constitutionally entitled to a hearing on their fitness before their children are removed from their custody.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/108497/stanley-v-illinois/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 108497
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re N.B.",
          "citation": "191 Ill. 2d 338, 730 N.E.2d 1086 (2000)",
          "court": "Illinois Supreme Court",
          "year": 2000,
          "date_decided": "2000-05-18",
          "holding": "The court held that the State must prove neglect by a preponderance of the evidence and, finding that two isolated displays of a mother's temper directed at no one in the household did not establish an injurious environment, reversed the appellate and circuit courts and ordered the neglect petitions dismissed.",
          "relevance": "The Illinois Supreme Court's original statement of the burden the State assumes the moment it files a wardship petition alleging neglect.",
          "quote": "The State bears the burden of proving neglect, dependence or abuse by a preponderance of the evidence, meaning proof that makes the condition more probable than not.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2001900/in-re-nb/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2001900
        },
        {
